What is the general opinion on nested looting of containers, that is
bags and boxes? Say I had a BoH in a sack. Wouldn't it be nice if I could

1. apply the sack
   in addition to [o] - take out/[i] - put in there's [a] on the menu
2. Do what? >> a - apply something from the sack
3. a - a bag of holding
4. Do what? >> o - take out ...
5. objects go straight to main inventory, BoH remains in the sack

I've just about written a small patch which does that and treats (imo)
correctly:

a) the time needed for such an operation (it's as if done step by step)

b) magical explosions (whole thing (outermost container) goes
kaboom--you didn't really remove the bag from the container so it eats
everything)

c) bags of tricks (well, the bag bites you, you ID it, and it hides in
the $container:)

d) topological excercises (outermost container is checked to prevent loops)

e) application of non-containers from within containers (this is
prevented--they don't even appear on the menu)

As these are multi-turn operations I can't decide whether one should get
encumbered (during the operation) if one applies a sack from within a
bag of holding but removes an object which would not encumber them. The
sack on the other hand is full of stuff and would encumber them but
after all one does not really remove it from the bag. It's just applying
within it, which for containers should be possible. What speaks against
is that perhaps this thing should be purely a shortcut and behave
exactly like the step-by-step operations. Except that it freezes you in
the process (that's not that bad for the single-nested case which would
be the most likely).

One thing that must be made for sure is updating encumberance during the
multi-turn operation if the removed object(s) are too heavy themselves.
Now it just uses up the right number of turns with the starting
encumberance whereas putting the containers back while encumbered would
be slower in the step-by-step version, I suppose. I must check and
improve on that part.

Anyway any ideas or suggestions? Is that something you would like to see
or do you perhaps consider it completely unnecessary?

